// 29_streaming.mty — v0.27 Track E (QoL gap #2): source-level streaming.
//
// `AnthropicClient.complete_stream()` worked at the Rust API since
// v0.26 but a Mighty `.mty` program couldn't iterate the resulting
// `MessageStream` cleanly — the iterator shape wasn't surfaced
// through the SIR interpreter or the prelude.
//
// v0.27 lands two surfaces on the Rust side:
//
//   * `MessageStream::next() -> Option[MessageDelta]` (async)
//   * `MessageStream::next_blocking() -> Option[MessageDelta]`
//
// Plus a `MessageStream` opaque ADT + a `next` entry in the prelude's
// permissive method table, so Mighty source can pull deltas from the
// stream through the typed handle.
//
// Build:
//   mty fmt examples/29_streaming.mty
//   mty check examples/29_streaming.mty
//
// We don't `mty run` here because exercising the live stream needs an
// `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`; the canonical end-to-end test lives in
// `crates/mty-stdlib/tests/llm_streaming_source.rs`.
// ----- Source-level streaming surface -----------------------------------
//
// `_drain_stream` takes an already-built `MessageStream` (the call
// site would be `client.messages_stream(req)`) and pulls a fixed
// number of deltas via the `next()` method. The permissive method
// table routes `next` through the SIR interpreter (which returns
// `None` for opaque receivers — the real iteration lives in
// `mty_stdlib::llm::streaming::MessageStream::next_blocking`).
//
// The bounded `for _i in 0..k` shape keeps the example deterministic
// at the parser level: `while let Some(d) = stream.next()` would be
// the canonical loop once the parser ships pattern bindings in `while`
// (v0.28). Until then this example demonstrates the call shape; the
// Rust-side test covers the full iteration semantics.
fn _drain_stream(stream: MessageStream, max_deltas: USize) -> USize {
  let mut count: USize = 0
  let mut i: USize = 0
  while i < max_deltas {
    let _delta = stream.next()
    count = count + 1
    i = i + 1
  }
  count
}

// `for delta in stream` would desugar onto `__mty_iter_next` for the
// arrays-and-ranges path. The typed `MessageStream` iteration goes
// through the explicit `next()` form above. This stub locks in the
// `for`-loop shape for arrays of deltas so the v0.28 iterator-protocol
// unification (where `MessageStream` impls the same iter trait the
// array path uses) has a regression hook.
fn _consume_via_for(deltas: &[I32]) -> USize {
  let mut count: USize = 0
  for _delta in deltas {
    count = count + 1
  }
  count
}

// ----- Entry point ------------------------------------------------------
//
// `main` exercises only the parser/checker surface — no LLM call. The
// helper fns above are the ones the test suite + future demo iterates
// against; `main` exists so `mty run` doesn't trap on a missing entry
// point when this example is exercised by the workspace's example
// sweep.
fn main() {
  log("29_streaming: source-level streaming surface")
}
